{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788198029,"nonce":"lYM8KyIlVWsUwOUPy6s0xrlhklc=","message":"CLOSED — audited, fixed, merged and verified in-game on BORGES.\nMerged `fda8e74`; audit fixes `60a4226`; DLL `09a4ace`.\n\n\nThe audit verified by member: `MapPawns.AllPawnsSpawned` is `=\u003e pawnsSpawned`,\nthe raw list, with no ordering promise; `MapPawns.RegisterPawn` sorts a\nDIFFERENT list (`pawnsInFactionSpawned`) and ties anyway on starting colonists;\n`PawnSerializer.Attention`'s mood term is one score point per mood point.\n\n**And it went further than the correction did:** `PawnVerbs` at the branch point\n(`307ca72`) ALREADY sorted attention-desc with a `thingIDNumber` tie-break. That\nmakes the ORIGINAL filed story — \"spawning a pawn reordered the roster\" —\n*impossible*, not merely unproven. Confirmed live below.\n\n`Pawn_PlayerSettings.displayOrder` is Class A as claimed — sentinel\n`UnsetDisplayOrder`, `Scribe_Values.Look`, and `ColonistBar.CheckRecacheEntries`\nwriting `Mathf.Max(...)+1` into the scribed field. **The branch does not read\nit**; the name appears in `Source/` only in a comment. It is now in `PawnSafe`'s\nClass A catalogue rather than only in DESIGN prose.\n\nThe fix adds **zero new Verse reads** — `thingIDNumber` is a plain public field.\n\n\n`order:\"id-asc\"` stabilises the **sequence**, not the **membership**. The cap\nstill cuts by attention (2.6, deliberately untouched), so above the cap the\nsurviving SET moves as moods move and `list[0]` can still name a different pawn.\nTrue without qualification only while `more == 0`. Now said in the class comment,\nin DESIGN, and in the acceptance's does-not-prove section, with `more` named as\nthe flag.\n\n**Demonstrated live at c4 below** — a `cap:2` read dropped pawn 218 out of the\nset entirely while keeping the two loudest.\n\n\n    a1  pawns {filter:\"colonist\"}\n          order:\"id-asc\"  selected_by:\"attention-desc\"  more:0\n          ids   = 215, 218, 221\n          ranks = 0, 1, 2\n\n    b1  dev:set-need {pawn:\"pawn:221\", need:\"Mood\", val:0.05}\n    b3  pawns {filter:\"colonist\"}\n          ids   = 215, 218, 221     \u003c- BYTE-IDENTICAL to a1\n          ranks = 0, 2, 1           \u003c- the score MOVED\n\nThat pair is the whole claim: crashing one pawn's mood permuted the attention\nranks and left the emitted sequence untouched. On the old assembly the sequence\nwould have moved with the score.\n\n    c2  dev:spawn-pawn {kind:\"Colonist\", faction:\"player\"}  -\u003e 27032\n    c3  pawns {filter:\"colonist\"}\n          ids = 215, 218, 221, 27032   total:4   \u003c- APPENDED, not permuted\n\nc3 is the direct refutation of the original diagnosis, in the game rather than in\nthe source.\n\n    c4  pawns {filter:\"colonist\", cap:2}\n          ids = 215, 221   more:2   order:\"id-asc\"   ranks = 0, 1\n\nc4 proves the id sort runs AFTER the attention cut — 2.6's selection survives —\nand simultaneously demonstrates the documented limit, since 218 sorts between\nthem by id and is gone.\n\n**Independently corroborated by 3.4's own run** on the merged DLL: `0.3c` and\n`0.3d` pass (`order` and `selected_by` as documented), and phase 0 now selects\nthe actor BY CAPABILITY — *\"actor A = 218 (Foxy) - selected by predicate, 2 of 3\ncolonists qualified\"*, with *\"not eligible as actor: Yun: Hauling disabled\"*.\nUnder the old `roster[0]` rule the actor would have been Yun, who fails six\nchecks for exactly that reason. **3.4: all 82 checks passed.**\n\nZero red errors across the entire bench session.\n\n\nCorrectly left alone, and the approach ports near-mechanically: `ThingVerbs`'\naddressable list carries `id`, sorts `ThingAttention` desc with an id tie-break,\nand publishes `things_order`; `rollups` is def-keyed and needs no change.\n\n\n`accept/1eb2262-*.md` A0 took its journal watermark with `{\"limit\":1}`.\n`JournalVerbs.Read` updates `last_seq` BEFORE the `since_seq` skip and breaks on\n`events.Count \u003e= limit` BEFORE the append, so that call reports the **second**\nline's seq. **The same flaw was live on `main` in `accept/3.4-pawn-orders.{ps1,py}`**\n— 3.4's red-error watermark has been near-zero all along, scanning the whole\njournal and able to charge a stale error to a clean run. Fixed in both, carried\nin merge `99a54e3`, verified live (`seq0 = 14` on a fresh load, not 2).\n\n\n- **The above-cap membership drift as a FAILURE.** c4 shows the set changing by\n  design with `more:2`. No run demonstrates an agent being burned by addressing\n  `list[0]` across two capped reads.\n- **A roster large enough to exercise the cap naturally** — `cap:2` was forced on\n  a 3-colonist colony. Nothing was run at the real cap of 20.\n- **`things`' identical fix**, which is `70ac258` and remains open.\n- **Multi-map colonies.** Every read above was single-map.\n- There is still **no `.ps1` driver** for `accept/1eb2262-stable-pawn-order.md`;\n  the above was hand-driven as raw file protocol.","files":null}]}